   "l": "All under heaven, all of them know the beautiful's being beautiful,\nand thereupon: the ugly.\nAll know the good's being good,\nand thereupon: the not-good.\nSo what-is and what-is-not give birth to one another,\nhard and easy complete one another,\nlong and short measure one another,\nhigh and low tip toward one another,\nnote and voice answer one another,\nbefore and after follow one another.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Once all under heaven knows the beautiful for beautiful, the ugly is there already; once all know the good for good, then the not-good. So what-is and what-is-not beget each other, hard and easy finish each other, long and short take each other's measure, high and low lean into each other, note and voice answer each other, before and after come one behind the other.",
   "n": "Three things here, and you will meet all three constantly. First, 之 takes a third job: in 美之為美 it stands between a subject and its predicate and makes the whole clause into a noun — 'beauty's being-beautiful', the thing that is known. In chapter 1 the same character was 'of' and then 'them'. Second, 為 here is doing the work of 'to be'. This language has no true copula — chapter 1's 非常道 needed no verb at all — and 為 is the nearest thing to one; the same character is also the ordinary verb 'to act', and read wèi it means 'for the sake of'. Every occurrence has to be decided. Third, 已 closes the clause as a particle, equivalent to 矣: it marks that a state has come about — 'and there it is' — which is why the English can say the ugly is there already. 斯 is taken here as 'then, thereupon'; it can equally be the demonstrative 'this', giving 'this is the ugly', and an unpunctuated text cannot settle between them. The six couplets are the plainest case in the book of the paired line as the unit of thought: two opposites and one verb that makes each depend on the other. 較 'measure against' is the received reading; the Mawangdui manuscripts read instead a word meaning 'to give shape', so that long and short shape each other rather than measure each other.",
   "p": true
  },
