   "i": "And so the sage goes about his work without forcing anything,",
   "n": "是以 is literally 'this-by': the preposition 以 'by reason of' with its object 是 'this' set in front of it instead of after. Classical Chinese fronts a pronoun object like this, and the phrase has hardened into a single connective, 'for this reason'. 無為 is the central term of the book, and it is not idleness: 為 is 'to act', 無 'to be without', so it is action that adds no force of its own. We render it acting without forcing everywhere. 聖人 is the sage — the word carries nothing of holiness in the Christian sense."
