   "l": "carries out the teaching of no-words;\nthe ten-thousand things rise up in it and he does not turn them away,\ngives birth and yet does not possess,\nacts and yet does not presume,",
   "v": true,
   "i": "and practises the teaching that uses no words. The ten thousand things rise up there and he turns none of them away; he gives them life and does not own them, acts and never presumes on it,",
   "n": "焉 is two words fused into one: 於 'in' plus 之 'it', hence 'in it, there'. It stands at the end of its clause, and it is the reason the line needs no object. Watch the subject while you are there: 萬物作焉 has the ten thousand things as its subject, but 不辭 swings back to the sage, and nothing in the writing marks the change. In 不言之教, 之 hangs a whole verb phrase — 不言 'not speaking' — on the noun 教 as its modifier. Where our text reads 不辭 'does not turn them away', the Mawangdui manuscripts read a word meaning 'does not start them off', which is a real difference of sense: refusing to reject the things, or refusing to initiate them."
  },
