   "i": "It is precisely because he does not settle into it that it never leaves him.",
   "n": "夫唯 ... 是以 ... is a fixed frame, and you will meet it again and again: 'it is precisely because X, that Y'. 夫 opening a sentence is read fú and clears the throat rather than saying anything; the same character read fū is 'a man'. And here is the commonest way to be quietly wrong in Classical Chinese: 去 means to leave, to depart from — the exact opposite of the modern Mandarin 'to go to'. What does not depart is the merit he refuses to sit in."
